  "worked_solution": "The general solution to the equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is the sum of the complementary function and a particular solution.\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ represents simple harmonic motion and can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=4\\,\u27f9\\,\\omega =2.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}\u20612t+B\\operatorname{sin}\u20612t.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t).$\n\nThe external force is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {t}^{2}+\\beta t+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nSubstituting ${y}_{p}$ into the differential equation and equating coefficients gives $\\alpha =2,\\beta =0,\\gamma =-1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=2{t}^{2}-1,\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}\u20612t+B\\operatorname{sin}\u20612t+2{t}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nSince the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, the initial conditions are $y(0)=0$ and ${y}^{\u2032}(0)=0.$\n\nSolving with these conditions for $A$ and $B$ gives\n\n$$\nA=1,\\,B=0.\n$$\n\nSo our solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\operatorname{cos}\u20612t+2{t}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nTo find the position of the particle after one second, we substitute into the equation $t=1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =\\operatorname{cos}\u20612(1)+2(1)^{2}-1 \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}\u20612+1)\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
